"""LLD tutorial sources and explicit LLD/HLD learning-path integration."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,html,textwrap
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
LESSONS=[
(367,'Parking lot: levels, gates and atomic slot assignment','parking-lot','ParkingLotDemo',
'Design one parking-lot process that can admit vehicles through multiple entry gates and close tickets at exit gates without assigning the same slot twice.',
'A car enters E1 on level 1. There are car slots on levels 0 and 1 and a bike slot on level 0. Our explicit policy chooses an exact-type slot nearest the entry level, breaking ties by slot ID. The car receives ticket 1 for the level-1 slot; an exit gate later closes that ticket and returns the slot to the free pool.',
[('Clarify the contract before classes','We model bike/car/truck kinds, numbered levels, entry/exit gates, active tickets and currently parked plates. This version uses exact-type fit; if motorcycles may occupy car spaces, introduce a documented compatibility rule. Full capacity returns no ticket. Duplicate active admission for the same plate and unknown/closed tickets are errors. Payment, pricing, reservation expiry and sensor reconciliation are extensions, not silently implemented features.'),('Assign responsibilities','Vehicle is immutable plate/type data. Slot has stable ID, level and kind. Gate has ID, level and direction. Ticket connects vehicle, assigned slot and entry gate. Lot owns the mutable free-slot and active-ticket indexes. Level is represented as a number on slots/gates; a richer product can promote it into a Level entity with physical capacity and display policies. UI panels and gate devices do not independently mutate the slot map.'),('Follow enter and exit','enter validates gate and vehicle, rejects an already parked plate, selects a free compatible slot, removes it from free state and records a ticket. All of those steps share one synchronized boundary. exit checks an exit gate and active ticket, removes the ticket/plate and releases that exact slot. A wrong-direction gate must fail before changing state. A repeated exit is rejected rather than incrementing availability twice.'),('Reason about concurrency and complexity','If two gates see one free slot, only one enter may finish with a ticket. The demo serializes the whole decision/update within one JVM. A free-slot scan costs O(s) for s available slots; indexes by level/type can improve selection but require consistent maintenance. Multiple application replicas need an authoritative database transaction/unique reservation or single assignment owner; synchronized does not coordinate separate processes.'),('Where authentication belongs','A real HTTP/gate integration authenticates devices/operators and authorizes permitted lot/gate actions outside this local object API. Knowing a ticket number should not permit arbitrary refunds or ticket edits. Persist ticket and occupancy changes transactionally if restart recovery is required. The demo deliberately keeps state in memory and does not claim durable parking control.')],
[('Validate entry','Check entry gate, vehicle kind and active plate before any slot mutation.'),('Select a slot','Choose exact kind, nearest entry level, then stable slot ID from the free pool.'),('Commit admission','Within the same lock, remove the slot and record vehicle plus ticket.'),('Close through exit','Validate exit gate and active ticket, then release exactly that slot once.')],
'The coarse lock favors correctness over throughput. Do not hold it while calling a payment terminal. A real payment/exit workflow needs recorded states and recovery for payment success followed by gate failure.',
'How would you prevent two entry gates on different servers from assigning the last car slot?'),
(368,'Tic Tac Toe: model turns, legal moves and terminal states','tic-tac-toe','TicTacToeDemo',
'Design a complete two-player 3×3 game using explicit board, player turn and game state. Choose Tic Tac Toe from the requested Chess-or-Tic-Tac-Toe exercise so the full rules can be implemented and tested.',
'X places at (0,0). O cannot overwrite that cell, and X cannot immediately play again. Later X fills the top row and the game becomes X_WON. All subsequent moves must be rejected. A different nine-move sequence fills the board without a win and becomes DRAW.',
[('Choose the objects and invariants','Mark is X or O; State is IN_PROGRESS, X_WON, O_WON or DRAW. Game owns a private 3×3 board, the current turn and the count of accepted moves. No UI or caller receives a mutable board reference. A move is accepted only when the game is active, the supplied player owns the turn, coordinates are in range and the cell is empty.'),('Execute one legal move','Validate before mutation, place the mark and increment move count. Check the affected row/column and both diagonals. A win takes precedence over draw on the final cell. If there is no win and all nine cells are filled, declare draw; otherwise switch the turn. A rejected move must not advance the turn or consume a move. This transition is synchronized in the lab.'),('Separate rules from interaction','A web controller translates an authorized player action into Game.move; a console or browser renders returned state. For online play, bind the authenticated user to an assigned mark server-side rather than accepting an arbitrary player field as authority. Persist moves or event history if reconnect/replay is required. A version guard can reject competing stale client moves across replicas.'),('Complexity and extension','For this fixed 3×3 board a move check has constant bounded work. A generalized n×n scan costs O(n) per move; row/column counters can reduce checking work when the rules allow it. Chess is a larger extension requiring piece-specific movement, obstruction, check/checkmate, castling and promotion; those are not provided by renaming this board. Avoid inheritance hierarchies with unused piece types for a game with only two marks.'),('Verify the rules, not only a happy path','The lab checks wrong turn, occupied cell, out-of-range coordinates, row and diagonal wins, draw and moves after termination. Add O wins, column/anti-diagonal wins and replay/version behavior when extending. Tests should assert that rejected commands leave state unchanged; they should not merely assert a status label after every method.')],
[('Receive a player move','Verify active game, assigned turn, coordinates and empty target cell.'),('Apply once','Place the mark and increase the count only after validation succeeds.'),('Evaluate the board','Check row, column and diagonals; win precedes draw.'),('Transition state','End on win/draw or switch the current turn; reject moves after termination.')],
'A local lock makes one Game instance atomic, not a shared online match across servers. A client-side win check is useful for display but the server must remain authoritative.',
'Why must a full-board winning move become X_WON/O_WON rather than DRAW?'),
(369,'LRU cache: expected O(1) lookup and eviction','lru-cache','LruCacheDemo',
'Build a bounded least-recently-used cache. Successful get and updating an existing key make it most recent. When inserting beyond capacity, evict the least recent key.',
'Capacity is two. put(1,A), put(2,B), get(1), put(3,C) must evict 2, because reading 1 made it newer. Updating 1 to A2 also refreshes it. A cache miss returns an explicit empty result; null keys/values are disallowed, and zero capacity is a disabled cache.',
[('Why a map or list alone is insufficient','A hash map quickly locates a key but does not provide the eviction order. A list preserves order but searching the whole list is O(n). Combine HashMap<K,Node> with a doubly linked list so the map finds the exact node and the node links allow constant pointer updates. Head/tail sentinels simplify empty and single-element cases.'),('Write the invariants','Every map entry identifies exactly one list node. Every non-sentinel list node has a matching map entry. head.next is most recent and tail.prev is least recent. Forward/back links agree; size never exceeds capacity at the end of a public operation. Store only references internally; decide whether mutable cached values themselves require defensive copies at a higher boundary.'),('Trace get and put','get finds a node, detaches it and moves it to the head. A miss does not create an entry. put on an existing key updates the value and moves the same node. put on a new key creates a node and, if over capacity, detaches tail.prev and removes its key from the map. Eviction must update both structures, not only the list.'),('Explain the O(1) claim precisely','List unlink/link work is O(1). Hash-table operations are expected/amortized O(1) under normal hash distribution; do not claim deterministic constant time for every possible hash collision. Hash-map resizing can cost more for an individual operation. Memory is O(capacity). A successful get mutates recency, so it needs the same synchronization protection as writes in this exact-LRU implementation.'),('Compare implementation choices','Java LinkedHashMap with access-order configuration and an eviction hook is a concise alternative; the explicit node implementation demonstrates the underlying design. The lab uses synchronized public operations for exact local order. High-throughput caches often use more elaborate or approximate policies. LRU is an eviction rule, not TTL, persistence, freshness, authorization or a distributed consistency guarantee.')],
[('Find the node','Use the map to locate a key, returning empty on a miss.'),('Refresh recency','Detach the found/updated node and insert it immediately after the head.'),('Enforce capacity','On new insert beyond capacity, remove tail.prev from both list and map.'),('Check structural safety','Map/list membership and links must agree after every completed operation.')],
'Independent synchronization of map and list leaves races. A cached permission or price can still be stale. Decide cache validity separately from which entry is evicted.',
'Why is get a write operation from the point of view of concurrency in an access-ordered LRU cache?'),
(370,'Thread-safe HashMap: define atomic operations first','thread-safe-hashmap','StripedMapDemo',
'Design a map whose documented single-key operations are safe under concurrent access, then explain which compound operations still need one atomic boundary. Use a fixed striped teaching implementation, not a claim to reimplement ConcurrentHashMap.',
'Eight threads each increment count 1,000 times. The result must be 8,000. A thread-safe get followed by a thread-safe put can still lose increments because the combined read-modify-write was not atomic. compute holds one key’s bucket write lock across the update.',
[('Start with the contract','Disallow null keys and values, use equals to distinguish colliding keys, define get-miss as null, allow compute returning null to remove a key, and provide an immutable consistent snapshot. Keys must keep stable hashCode/equals while stored. Thread-safe container structure does not make a mutable value object automatically safe.'),('Partition synchronization','Choose a fixed number of buckets. Each owns an ordinary HashMap and a read/write lock. Hash the key with floorMod to safely handle negative hashes including Integer.MIN_VALUE. Reads take a bucket read lock; puts and compute take its write lock. Operations in different buckets can proceed independently, while colliding keys intentionally contend on one bucket.'),('Make read-modify-write atomic','compute acquires the bucket write lock, reads old value, calculates replacement and updates/removes before unlocking in finally. Keep callbacks short, nonblocking and free of calls back into this map; calling across buckets or requesting a snapshot inside a callback can deadlock. An application needing external I/O should design that operation outside the lock with explicit race semantics.'),('Define global operations honestly','A snapshot acquires all bucket read locks in a fixed order and releases in reverse, copying values into an immutable map. That blocks writers and is O(number of entries + stripes), unlike the expected single-key lookup cost. Per-bucket HashMap resizing is protected by that bucket lock; this demo does not dynamically change the stripe array or offer lock-free reads.'),('Production comparison','Prefer Java ConcurrentHashMap when its documented operations match the need; use compute, merge or putIfAbsent instead of assuming get-plus-put is atomic. Do not describe modern ConcurrentHashMap as merely the old fixed Segment design. Its iteration/aggregate semantics differ from this lab’s all-bucket snapshot. A distributed key-value store additionally needs network, durability, replication and partition behavior.')],
[('Select a bucket','Use a stable key hash and floorMod; colliding keys still require equals checks.'),('Acquire correct lock','Read for get, write for put/compute; always release in finally.'),('Complete one atomic change','Read old value and write/remove the replacement within the same bucket lock.'),('Coordinate a snapshot','Acquire all bucket read locks in fixed order to make a consistent copied view.')],
'Stripes improve parallelism only for keys spread across buckets. Hot keys remain contended. This in-memory map has no restart recovery and coordinates no separate processes.',
'Why can individually thread-safe get and put methods still produce a wrong concurrent counter?'),
(371,'Rate limiter: token-bucket LLD and distributed boundary','rate-limiter','TokenBucketDemo',
'Implement a token bucket with injected time, atomic token accounting and tenant selection; then connect it to a cluster-wide admission design. The runnable Java bucket is local to one process.',
'A tenant starts with capacity 2 and refill 1 token/second. Two requests succeed immediately, the third is rejected. After half a second there is still no full token; after another half second one request succeeds. Fifty simultaneous requests against a frozen ten-token bucket admit exactly ten.',
[('Define policy and objects','Bucket owns capacity, refill rate, tokens, last-observed time and a monotonic clock supplier. Limiter holds a fixed registry of configured tenant buckets so arbitrary keys cannot grow memory without bound in this demo. Each positive request cost consumes that many tokens; a cost above available tokens is rejected. Unknown tenants are rejected. Identity must already have been authenticated by the caller boundary.'),('Refill and consume atomically','Inside one synchronized operation, compute elapsed monotonic time, add elapsed*rate capped at capacity, then compare and subtract cost. Keep fractional tokens so half-second waits accumulate correctly. The lab injects an AtomicLong clock to test without sleeps. System.nanoTime is appropriate for local elapsed time; separate machines do not share a comparable nanoTime origin.'),('Connect to API behavior','On allow, continue to the backend, which still checks object-level authorization and resource capacity. On deny, return an appropriate HTTP 429 contract with retry guidance. Do not hold the bucket lock across backend processing. Rate limits bound arrivals; they do not replace in-flight limits, bounded queues, timeouts or backpressure.'),('Make cluster-wide semantics explicit','Deploying this Java map on five independent servers creates five budgets. For a stricter shared per-tenant limit, route the key to one authoritative limiter owner or perform refill/consume atomically in a shared store/script with an explicit time/failover policy. Local/regional token allocations can reduce coordination if the product accepts a defined overshoot budget. Do not label a process-local synchronized block globally scalable by itself.'),('Handle failure and key lifecycle','Choose fail-open or fail-closed per endpoint risk when the limiter store is unavailable; monitor the fallback. Bound key cardinality and idle lifetime in a dynamic registry. Concurrent bucket eviction/recreation must not reset an active budget and grant extra bursts. Backoff retry traffic and test hot tenants so the limiter does not become the bottleneck it was meant to prevent.')],
[('Resolve trusted tenant','Look up a configured policy from authenticated identity, not an arbitrary header.'),('Refill with elapsed time','Add fractional tokens up to capacity under the same lock as consumption.'),('Consume or reject','Atomically subtract only when sufficient tokens exist; return a clear decision.'),('Choose distributed ownership','For multiple servers, coordinate the budget and document outage/overshoot behavior.')],
'The double-based lab is an explanatory local algorithm, not a precision/accounting guarantee for financial quotas. Production designs can use bounded integer/fixed-point accounting and must define clock, failover and TTL semantics.',
'If the same tenant has a capacity-20 bucket independently on five servers, why can it receive a much larger initial burst?')]

def diagram(path,title,steps,failure):
 esc=html.escape
 svg=[f'<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="960" height="960" viewBox="0 0 960 960" role="img" aria-labelledby="title desc"><title id="title">{esc(title)}</title><desc id="desc">Four-step illustrative design walkthrough.</desc>', '<defs><marker id="arrow" markerWidth="10" markerHeight="10" refX="8" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L8,3 L0,6" fill="#38bdf8"/></marker></defs>','<rect width="960" height="960" rx="18" fill="#0f172a"/>',f'<text x="35" y="48" fill="#f8fafc" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="24">{esc(title)}</text>','<text x="35" y="80" fill="#cbd5e1" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16">Follow the numbered responsibilities; process-local and distributed boundaries differ.</text>']
 for i,(label,body) in enumerate(steps):
  y=110+i*168;svg += [f'<rect x="35" y="{y}" width="890" height="142" rx="12" fill="#1e293b" stroke="#38bdf8"/>',f'<text x="55" y="{y+32}" fill="#7dd3fc" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="21">{i+1}. {esc(label)}</text>']
  for j,line in enumerate(textwrap.wrap(body,92)):svg.append(f'<text x="55" y="{y+62+j*22}" fill="#f1f5f9" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17">{esc(line)}</text>')
  if i<3:svg.append(f'<path d="M480,{y+144} L480,{y+165}" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>')
 svg.append('<text x="35" y="818" fill="#fbbf24" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19">Boundary / failure to explain</text>')
 for j,line in enumerate(textwrap.wrap(failure,101)):svg.append(f'<text x="35" y="{848+j*21}" fill="#e2e8f0" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16">{esc(line)}</text>')
 svg.append('</svg>');(ROOT/path).write_text('\n'.join(svg),encoding='utf-8')

def build():
 from system_design_tutorials import content_blocks
 file=ROOT/'data/questions.json';questions=json.loads(file.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
 def upsert(q):
  old=next((x for x in questions if x['id']==q['id']),None)
  if old is not None:
   assert old.get('guidePath')==q['guidePath'],('ID collision',q['id']);old.update(q)
  else:questions.append(q)
 for ident,title,slug,lab,concept,scenario,sections,steps,failure,check in LESSONS:
  source=f'examples/lld/{lab}.java';code=(ROOT/source).read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig')
  image=f'docs/lld/diagrams/{slug}.svg';guide=f'docs/lld/{slug}.md';diagram(image,title,steps,failure)
  q={'id':ident,'category':'Low-Level Design — LLD','question':title+'?','priority':'High','answer':concept,'explanation':concept,'why':'Start with the state and invariants, then choose classes, operations and synchronization.','example':scenario,'detailStatus':'Expanded','lessonSections':[{'title':a,'explanation':b} for a,b in sections],'walkthroughSteps':[{'title':a,'explanation':b} for a,b in steps],'tradeoffs':failure,'knowledgeCheck':check,'diagram':' → '.join(a for a,b in steps),'diagramImage':image,'guidePath':guide,'labPath':source,'labType':'Java 21','code':code,'runCommand':f'javac -d .pattern-classes {source}; java -cp .pattern-classes {lab}'}
  upsert(q)
  parts=[f'# {title}\n',concept+'\n','## Follow a concrete operation\n',scenario+'\n']
  for a,b in sections:parts.extend([f'## {a}\n',b+'\n'])
  parts.extend([f'![{title}](diagrams/{slug}.svg)\n','## Trace the responsibilities\n'])
  parts.extend(f'{i}. **{a}:** {b}\n' for i,(a,b) in enumerate(steps,1))
  parts.extend(['## Runnable Java 21 example\n','This standard-library lab runs locally. It does not deploy a backend, persist data, contact a bank or coordinate multiple servers.\n',f'[Source](../../{source})\n',f'```java\n{code.rstrip()}\n```\n',f'```powershell\n{q["runCommand"].replace("; ",chr(10))}\n```\n','Expected: PASS after assertions verify the stated invariants.\n','## Trade-offs and failure handling\n',failure+'\n','## Interview check\n',check+'\n','[LLD and HLD learning paths](../lld-hld-learning-path.md)\n'])
  if slug=='lru-cache':parts.append('[Java access-ordered LinkedHashMap contract](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/LinkedHashMap.html)\n')
  if slug=='thread-safe-hashmap':parts.append('[Java ConcurrentHashMap contract](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/ConcurrentHashMap.html)\n')
  (ROOT/guide).write_text('\n'.join(parts),encoding='utf-8')
 kv_steps=[('Authenticate and route','Derive tenant/key and resolve the current authoritative shard owner.'),('Order the write','The leader orders a validated operation in its consensus-controlled durable log.'),('Commit under the protocol','Use the chosen majority/commit rules, apply state and return a versioned result.'),('Read with declared consistency','Establish read authority for a strong read; do not silently substitute a stale follower.')]
 kv_image='docs/system-design/diagrams/distributed-key-value-store.svg'
 diagram(kv_image,'Distributed key-value store: one ordered key history',kv_steps,'A timeout can hide a committed write. Preserve operation identity/version semantics. A minority cannot promise a strong write merely to stay responsive.')
 for ident,slug,title,category,image,steps in [(372,'distributed-key-value-store','Design a distributed key-value store','High-Level Design — Distributed Key-Value Store',kv_image,kv_steps),(373,'notification-platform','Design a notification system with fan-out and retries','High-Level Design — Notifications','docs/system-design/diagrams/notification-platform.svg',[('Record durable intent','Authenticate the producer and persist authorized event/recipient intent.'),('Fan out permitted channels','Apply current preferences and create channel-specific work with stable IDs.'),('Send and reconcile','Workers respect provider quotas, retry bounded transient failures and track unknown outcomes.'),('Observe delivery separately','Accepted intent, provider acceptance, delivery and read are different states.')])]:
  guide=f'docs/system-design/{slug}.md';text=(ROOT/guide).read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig')
  upsert({'id':ident,'category':category,'question':title+'?','priority':'High','answer':text.split('\n\n')[1],'detailStatus':'Expanded','contentBlocks':content_blocks(text),'guidePath':guide,'learningTitle':title,'walkthroughSteps':[{'title':a,'explanation':b} for a,b in steps],'knowledgeCheck':'Which acknowledgement is durable, which owner may decide, and what happens after a timeout?','diagram':' → '.join(a for a,b in steps),'diagramImage':image})
 file.write_text(json.dumps(questions,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n',encoding='utf-8')
 print('Added five LLD labs and two missing HLD portal lessons; existing HLD tutorials are reused.')

if __name__=='__main__':build()
